{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788352136,"nonce":"COqeJqNPDbC3O93r+ibm6xoDUPI=","message":"Fixed on branch `fix/5eba561-transcript-rotate` (5 commits, pure Python — no `Source/`, no DLL).\n\n`9d6855f` rwa client · `425c20e` accept 4.2 · `03f5ecd` rwa/selftest · `b797fad` README + DESIGN · `9e3b4e5` PLAY-LOOP + 4.2 doc\n\n\n1. **Envelope, not a traceback.** `Transcript.step` raises a typed\n   `TranscriptFull`; `send` catches it at the point it already claims the step\n   directory — before the inbox write — and returns\n   `envelope(op, False, code=\"rwa-transcript-full\", …)`, the same constructor\n   every other client-side failure uses. `rwa.sent: false` is the load-bearing\n   field, and the refusal is still appended to the run's `log.ndjson` via the\n   existing `note()` path, the same way `rwa-game-down` is.\n2. **Rotates by default.** `\u003crun\u003e` is segment 0, then `\u003crun\u003e-s01`, `-s02`, …\n   `--no-rotate` / `RWA_NO_ROTATE` is the opt-out and is now the only way to\n   reach the cap at all.\n3. **Chain links.** Each segment's `meta.json` carries `base`, `segment`,\n   `cap`, `prev`, `next`. `accept/4.2-play-loop.py --transcript` is repeatable,\n   takes several values, accepts a glob, and follows the links in both\n   directions — actually changed, not asserted.\n4. **Cap re-justified**, documented in `rwa/README.md` §Transcripts under \"The\n   999-step cap, and segments\", which is new — that file had never mentioned a\n   limit.\n5. **`selftest.sh` covers it** — section 9, 30 new assertions, all offline.\n\n\n**The cap stays at 999 and the number is the width of the step counter.** Not a\ndirectory-size guess. Every consumer orders steps by SORTING directory names —\n`cmd_replay`, `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`, and any `ls` a human runs — the names\nare zero-padded to three digits, and `1000-ping` sorts BEFORE `999-ping`.\nWidening the field would silently reorder every transcript already on disk, and\nit buys nothing once the client keeps going past the end of it. Also: the old\nmessage said \"1000 steps\" and refused at 999, because the counter starts at\n001. The cap is 999 and now says so.\n\n**The suffix is `-sNN`, not the `-002` the issue proposed.** `m1-20260901`,\n`-s00`, `-s01`, `-s02`, `-s03` are on disk right now and a consumer has to walk\nwhat exists; a prettier scheme would have orphaned the one run this has ever\nhappened to. Consequence, accepted deliberately: the shell workaround's `-s00`\nand the bare base directory both read as segment 0. That is not a conflict —\nthe bare one ran first and sorts first on the name tiebreak, which is the order\nit actually ran in. New runs rotate to `-s01` first, so nothing new produces a\n`-s00`.\n\n**Exit code 2, not 1.** `rwa-transcript-full` joins the usage errors. Nothing\nreached the mod, so it must not share the exit code documented as \"the mod said\n`ok:false`\". A driver branching on `1` would otherwise read a full directory as\na refusal from the colony.\n\n**Opening a run resolves to its LAST segment.** Every `rwa` call is its own\nprocess. Without this the call after a rotation would reopen the full first\nsegment and rotate again — one new segment per command for the rest of the run.\nAsserted in the self-test.\n\n**A link-less chain is repaired once, on first use.** The segments the shell\nworkaround wrote carry only `run`/`client`/`started`, so `m1-20260901` cannot\nbe walked forward at all. Opening the run derives the links across the whole\nchain, gated on the tail predating this format so it runs once and never on the\nhot path. Metas are merged, never clobbered, and written only when a field\nactually changes.\n\n**`rwa replay` still replays ONE segment.** Re-sending a multi-day run because\nthe directory happened to be its first segment is not a thing to do by\naccident. It now warns and names the successor when the segment it was handed\nhas one.\n\n**Auditing the head of a chain is the dangerous shape, not the incomplete one.**\nOn the real run:\n\n```\n$ python3 accept/4.2-play-loop.py RUNS/m1-20260901 --transcript transcripts/m1-20260901\nPASS  transcript-chain      1 segment(s): m1-20260901\nPASS  advance-invariants    113 advances within policy\n\n$ python3 accept/4.2-play-loop.py RUNS/m1-20260901 --transcript 'transcripts/m1-20260901*'\nPASS  transcript-chain      5 segment(s): m1-20260901 -\u003e m1-20260901-s00 -\u003e m1-20260901-s01 -\u003e m1-20260901-s02 -\u003e m1-20260901-s03\nFAIL  advance-invariants    two consecutive 0-tick advances (wedge rule violated)\n```\n\nA green line over a fifth of a run is worse than no line, so the new\n`transcript-chain` check always names the segments it read and FAILs a spec\nthat matched nothing — every other check goes green on an empty audit. (Both\nFAILs on the full chain are that run's own play defects, not this change.)\n\n\nNo bench. Everything above is `fakebench.py` and synthetic roots; a live\nrotation mid-run — 999 real round trips against a running game — has not\nhappened and is the orchestrator's to run if it is wanted. The three\n`transcripts/m1-20260901*` directories in the main checkout were only READ;\ntheir links are still missing, and one `RWA_RUN=m1-20260901 rwa ping` against\nthe real transcript root would derive them.\n\n\n`1de2fbe` (p2) — `rwa/selftest.sh` fails 28 checks when run from `rwa/`, which\nis what its own README tells you to do. Section 13 names `templates/*.ir.json`\nrelative to the cwd while every other path in the file resolves from `$0`.\nPre-existing; confirmed on `main`.","files":null}]}